Title: cmd/compile: reverse bounds check elimination

Body: This code has two bounds checks but I think it should only need one. This is not entirely obvious but seems within the realm of possibility:

 [CODE_BLOCK] package p

func f(a []uint32, u uint64) {
 i := len(a) // i >= 0, i 0 {
 // 0 = 0
 a[i+1] = d2 // 0 = 0
 for u > 0 {
 // 0 = 0
 for u > 0 {
 // 0 <= i <= len(a)
 var d1 uint32
 var d2 uint32
 u, d1 = u/10, uint32(u%10)
 u, d2 = u/10, uint32(u%10)
 if 0 <= i {
 a[i+1] = d2 // need to check i+1 <= len(a)-1
 a[i] = d1 // now 0 <= i < len(a)-2, no check needed
 i += 2 // i <= len(a), has not wrapped negative
 }
 }
}
 [/CODE_BLOCK] 

In the first example I have not yet found an obvious test to add that explains the problem. :-)

Title: html/template: JS context is not reset when template literal closes

Body: Easiest described by example: [URL]

 [CODE_BLOCK] t1 := template.Must(template.New("").Parse(
 " let x = y/{{.}}/b; \n"))
t1.Execute(os.Stdout, "alert`1`")
// Template parameter is quoted:
// let x = y/"alert`1`"/b; 

t2 := template.Must(template.New("").Parse(
 " let x = `a`/{{.}}/b; \n"))
t2.Execute(os.Stdout, "alert`1`")
// Template parameter is not quoted:
// let x = `a`/alert`1`/b; 
 [/CODE_BLOCK] 

This occurs because when the JavaScript template literal ``` `a` ``` closes, the parser remains in the wrong (regexp) context, and the following value is not escaped.

This was reported to us as a potential vulnerability. This is bug, but not something we consider a security bug. JavaScript parsing is highly complex, and html/template implements only a subset of it. We generally recommend not putting template actions in complex JavaScript programs, since contextual escaping bugs of this nature are highly likely.

